---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 5: Loops and Repetition with Arduino">
    <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 py-12">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
            <div class="text-center mb-8">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center mb-4">
                    <div class="bg-blue-500 text-white rounded-full w-16 h-16 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-2xl mr-4">5</div>
                    <div class="text-left">
                        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800">Lesson 5: Loops and Repetition with Arduino</h1>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Use `loop()` and `for` loops to repeat actions clearly and on purpose</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-blue-600 font-medium">32-Lesson Course • Semester 1</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-4 rounded-lg mb-8">
                <p class="text-blue-900 font-medium">Lesson 4 taught you how to make decisions with `if` and `else`. Now you will learn how Arduino repeats actions. Repetition is one of the most important ideas in programming because computers are very good at doing the same kind of task again and again.</p>
            </div>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Learning Objectives</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">By the end of this lesson, you will:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Understand that `loop()` automatically repeats forever</li>
                            <li>Use a `for` loop to repeat an action a specific number of times</li>
                            <li>Trace what happens during each repetition</li>
                            <li>Combine loops with LEDs, delays, and simple conditionals</li>
                            <li>Recognize when repeated code should become a loop</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Key ideas:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Repetition saves time and reduces repeated code</li>
                            <li>`loop()` is built into every Arduino sketch</li>
                            <li>`for` loops are useful when you know how many times to repeat</li>
                            <li>A loop variable changes as the repetitions continue</li>
                            <li>Loops often work together with `if` statements</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">What Does `loop()` Do?</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Every basic Arduino sketch has `setup()` and `loop()`. `setup()` runs once when the program starts. `loop()` runs again and again for as long as the board has power.</p>
                <div class="bg-slate-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-4">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Simple idea</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700">If you place a line of code inside `loop()`, Arduino does not run it only one time. It keeps repeating that code over and over.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  Serial.println("Arduino is repeating this line.");
  delay(1000);
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">This message prints once each second because `loop()` keeps starting over after it reaches the end.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Why Loops Matter</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Without loops, you would have to write the same lines again and again. That becomes messy very quickly.</p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">Without a loop</h3>
                        <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
delay(500);
digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
delay(500);
digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
delay(500);
digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
delay(500);`}</pre>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-3">With a loop</h3>
                        <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  delay(500);
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  delay(500);
}`}</pre>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Understanding a `for` Loop</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A `for` loop repeats a block of code a specific number of times. It is very useful when you already know how many repetitions you want.</p>
                <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`for (starting value; condition; change) {
  // code to repeat
}`}</pre>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mt-4">
                    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Start</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">`int i = 0` creates the loop variable and gives it a starting value.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Condition</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">`i < 5` means “keep going while `i` is less than 5.”</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-slate-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Change</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">`i++` adds 1 after each repetition.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Blink an LED Five Times</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">This is a classic beginner use of a `for` loop. The code repeats the blink pattern exactly five times.</p>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 text-blue-900">
                    Use an LED on pin `8` with a resistor to ground.
                </div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int ledPin = 8;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
    delay(300);
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
    delay(300);
  }

  delay(2000);
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">What to notice</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-700">
                        <li>The blink code runs five times because `i` takes the values `0, 1, 2, 3, 4`</li>
                        <li>When `i` becomes `5`, the condition `i < 5` is false</li>
                        <li>Then the program continues to the next line after the loop</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Loops and Conditionals Together</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Loops and conditionals often work together. For example, a button can decide whether an LED should blink multiple times.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        �� Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int ledPin = 8;
const int buttonPin = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);

  if (buttonState == LOW) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
      digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
      delay(200);
      digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
      delay(200);
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Here the button decision uses `if`, and the repeated blinking uses a `for` loop.</p>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 1</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Forgetting that `loop()` already repeats forever.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 2</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Writing a `for` loop condition that never becomes false.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 3</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Not tracing the loop variable one step at a time.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 4</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Repeating many lines manually instead of using a loop.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Hands-On Activity: Button Makes the LED Blink</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Build a simple circuit where pressing the button makes the LED blink three times. If the button is not pressed, the LED stays off.</p>
                <div class="bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4 rounded-lg mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2">Required parts</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-yellow-900">
                        <li>Arduino Uno</li>
                        <li>Breadboard</li>
                        <li>1 LED</li>
                        <li>1 resistor (`220Ω` or `330Ω`)</li>
                        <li>1 pushbutton</li>
                        <li>Jumper wires</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-slate-50 p-5 rounded-lg mb-6">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Activity steps</h3>
                    <ol class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 list-decimal list-inside">
                        <li>Wire the LED to digital pin `8` and ground through a resistor</li>
                        <li>Wire the button to digital pin `2` and ground</li>
                        <li>Set the button pin to `INPUT_PULLUP`</li>
                        <li>Use `if` to check whether the button is pressed</li>
                        <li>Use a `for` loop to blink the LED three times</li>
                        <li>Test the circuit and count the blinks carefully</li>
                    </ol>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-blue-900">
                    Extension: change the loop so the LED blinks five times, then change the speed by editing the delay values.
                </div>
            </section>

            <section class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-8 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-blue-600 mb-4">Assessment and Homework</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Lesson 5 quiz topics</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>What `loop()` does</li>
                            <li>How a `for` loop works</li>
                            <li>Tracing the loop variable</li>
                            <li>Using loops with LEDs and delays</li>
                            <li>Combining loops with `if` statements</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Homework ideas</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Write a `for` loop that prints numbers 1 through 5</li>
                            <li>Change a blink loop from 3 repetitions to 10</li>
                            <li>Explain in words what `i++` means</li>
                            <li>Practice deciding when you need repetition in a program</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-green-900">
                    When a program repeats the same task many times, stop and ask: should this be a loop?
                </div>
            </section>

            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mt-8">
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson4" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        ← Previous: Lesson 4
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div class="text-center">
                    <a href="/programming/quizzes32/Lesson5" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 6px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 500; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Take Quiz 📝
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson6" style="background-color: #2563eb; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #1d4ed8; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#1d4ed8'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#2563eb'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Next: Lesson 6 →
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<script>
declare global {
    interface Window {
        copyCode: (button: HTMLButtonElement) => void;
    }
}

const lesson5Window = window as Window & typeof globalThis;

lesson5Window.copyCode = function(button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const parentElement = button.parentElement;
        if (!parentElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeElement = parentElement.querySelector('code');
        if (!codeElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeText = codeElement.textContent || codeElement.innerText;

        if (navigator.clipboard && window.isSecureContext) {
            navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                showCopyFeedback(button, true);
            }).catch(() => {
                fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
            });
        } else {
            fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
        }
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
};

function fallbackCopy(text: string, button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const textarea = document.createElement('textarea');
        textarea.value = text;
        textarea.style.position = 'absolute';
        textarea.style.left = '-9999px';
        textarea.style.top = '0';
        textarea.setAttribute('readonly', '');
        document.body.appendChild(textarea);

        textarea.select();
        textarea.setSelectionRange(0, 99999);

        const successful = document.execCommand('copy');
        document.body.removeChild(textarea);

        showCopyFeedback(button, successful);
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
}

function showCopyFeedback(button: HTMLButtonElement, success: boolean) {
    const originalText = button.textContent;
    const originalBg = button.style.backgroundColor;

    if (success) {
        button.textContent = '✅ Copied!';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#10b981';
    } else {
        button.textContent = '❌ Failed';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#dc2626';
    }

    setTimeout((): void => {
        button.textContent = originalText;
        button.style.backgroundColor = originalBg || '#2563eb';
    }, 2000);
}
</script>
